const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How often should I feed a kitten by age?',
    a: 'Very young weaned kittens often need three to four meals per day; many households continue three meals until about six months, then move toward two measured meals as they approach adulthood. Nursing kittens feed on demand from the queen. Orphaned neonates need veterinary-guided bottle schedules—not cow’s milk. Exact timing depends on age, health, and your veterinarian’s advice.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much should a kitten eat per day?',
    a: 'Amount follows calories more than bowl size. A common teaching approach multiplies Resting Energy Requirement (70 × kg^0.75) by a kitten factor around 2.5. A 1.5 kg (3.3 lb) kitten lands near 95 kcal RER × 2.5 ≈ 237 kcal/day as a starting estimate. Adjust by body condition and growth; free-feeding calorie-dense dry food easily causes obesity.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When do kittens switch to adult cat food?',
    a: 'Most cats stay on a complete growth (“kitten”) diet until about 12 months of age. Large-breed cats such as Maine Coons may benefit from longer growth feeding while they fill out—ask your veterinarian. Transition gradually over about seven to ten days. Do not switch early just to save money; adult maintenance food is not formulated for growth.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I free-feed my kitten?',
    a: 'Some kittens self-regulate, but many overeat dry food left out all day, especially after neutering. Measured meals make appetite changes visible and protect against early obesity. If you free-feed, weigh weekly and feel for ribs. Wet food measured meals plus puzzle feeders often work better for indoor kittens.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my kitten is orphaned or not gaining weight?',
    a: 'Failure to thrive, diarrhea, or constant crying needs prompt veterinary care. Kitten milk replacer type, volume, and frequency must be individualized; homemade milk recipes and cow’s milk cause problems. Do not attempt tube feeding unless a clinician has trained you. Underweight kittens are emergencies more often than owners expect.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should kittens eat wet food, dry food, or both?',
    a: 'Either can work if the label states complete and balanced for growth (or all life stages including growth). Wet food adds moisture; dry food is convenient for frequent small meals. Many veterinarians like a mix. Keep total daily calories inside the target and ensure fresh water is always available.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do nursing mother cats need a kitten feeding schedule too?',
    a: 'Queens in late pregnancy and lactation usually need free access to a growth/lactation diet because energy needs soar. That is different from measured kitten meals. Coordinate queen and litter feeding with your veterinarian so kittens wean smoothly without the mother losing unsafe amounts of weight.',
  },
